// A field declared `list<string>` answers that text, and a field declared
// `list` answers the bare container: `declaredFieldType` reads the type the
// source wrote, so the first carries its element kind to every later read of
// `items` and the second carries nothing. `declaredFieldType` used to deny both
// and return "" for them, calling `list<string>` punctuation the type column
// does not carry while the column carried `list` for every literal.
//
// A list literal is not a field: the source wrote its elements, not their type,
// so `lowerExpression` and `typeOfExpression` agree on the bare `list`. The
// index read `xs[i]` is not given the answer the `.get(i)` call has, and that
// give-up is the one tests/frontend/index_write.orb names; the census counts it
// as `expression cannot be lowered to TIR: IndexAccess`. Iterating a list with
// `for x in xs` is the same missing answer, pinned by
// tests/frontend/list_iteration.orb.
import "../../compiler/tir/entry.orb"

fn main() {
    val source = "model Bag { items: list<string> }  model Box { tags: list }  fn take_items(bag: Bag) -> list { return bag.items }  fn take_tags(b: Box) -> list { return b.tags }"
    print(compileFrontendSource("fixture", source))
}
